Topological Dynamical Membrane Universe (TDMU) Model v2.341
——A Cosmological Framework Based on Discrete Combinatorial Networks

Author: Ma Jiangping

Abstract

The standard cosmological model (ΛCDM) relies on three exogenous assumptions—the singularity, the inflaton field, and dark matter particles with a dark energy field—whose physical nature remains unexplained within the framework of known physics. This paper proposes an alternative framework: the universe is modeled as a topological interface (membrane) emerging from a discrete combinatorial network. This network consists of nodes and directed edges, and its sole dynamics is local reconnection.

Within this framework: space is the ensemble of distance relations among structures on the membrane; time is the cumulative count of reconnection events; matter consists of stable loop-woven subgraphs (topological knots), the recursive growth of which is truncated by the current energy scale; gravity is a statistical effect of the total number of shared links; light is the propagation of orientation perturbations of shared links; nuclear transformations occur through defect-complementary weaving and forced reconnection (four channels); elemental abundances are determined by a three-factor criterion (synthesis probability, continued polymerization probability, and fission probability); and astrophysical evolution is a dynamical game between Path I and Path II at different scales within an accretion-dissipation cycle, with stellar types depending on total accreted mass and the state of the medium.

This framework does not employ a singularity (replaced by a finite critical loop density), inflation (replaced by the initial conditions of membrane emergence), dark matter particles (replaced by the shared-link convergence effect of supermassive topological stars), or a dark energy field (replaced by the topological tension coupling between the membrane and the external substrate). All observable cosmological phenomena—homogeneous background, large-scale structure, accelerated expansion, and elemental abundance distribution—emerge from the same set of geometric rules.

This paper provides a complete system of postulates, core derivations, an astrophysical evolutionary spectrum, and testable predictions.

1. Introduction
1.1 Exogenous Assumptions of the Standard Cosmological Model
The contemporary standard model of cosmology (ΛCDM) has achieved remarkable observational success, but its theoretical architecture relies on three exogenous assumptions:

Singularity: The universe is born from a mathematical singularity where spacetime curvature diverges. At this point, density, temperature, and curvature all tend to infinity, and general relativity breaks down. A physical theory that contains an indescribable singularity at its core indicates that the theory is not complete.

Inflation: To explain the homogeneity and flatness of the universe, the standard model introduces a scalar field that undergoes superluminal expansion in the very early universe. This field has no known physical counterpart, and the form of its potential requires artificial tuning to match observational data.

Dark Matter and Dark Energy: To explain galactic dynamics and the accelerated expansion of the universe, the standard model introduces two unknown components that constitute 95% of the total energy of the universe. Dark matter particles have never been detected in Earth-based experiments; the physical nature of dark energy is completely unknown.

The common feature of these three assumptions is that they are not derived from deeper principles but are entities added layer by layer to fit observational data.

1.2 The Path of This Paper
The Topological Dynamical Membrane Universe (TDMU) model starts from a set of extremely parsimonious postulates: only nodes, directed edges, shared links, and a reconnection count. From these underlying definitions, all the above phenomena are derived or reformulated. The tasks of this paper are:

To present the complete system of postulates (Parts I to II)

To establish the emergence mechanism of material structure (Part III)

To define the four channels of nuclear transformation and the three-factor criterion for elemental abundance (Parts IV to V)

To establish the geometry of shared links and the gravity-light decoupling (Part VI)

To construct the astrophysical evolutionary spectrum and cosmological framework (Parts VII to VIII)

To list testable predictions (Part IX)

Part I: Ontology
Postulate 1: The Substrate
There exists a discrete, background-free combinatorial network ℳ. It consists of nodes and directed edges connecting the nodes.

This network is not embedded in any space. It does not evolve in any pre-existing time. The sole primitive relation between nodes and edges is "connected" or "not connected." The sole primitive attribute of an edge is its direction—pointing from one node to another.

The sole dynamics of the substrate is local reconnection: when two edges meet at a node, they can disconnect and reconnect according to a direction rule. Reconnection is local—it involves only a few edges sharing the same node and does not involve distant structures.

Direction Rule: On any closed path, when traversing the path, edges consistent with the traversal direction are counted as forward, and edges opposite to the traversal direction are counted as backward. The algebraic sum of the directedness of all edges is conserved under local reconnection operations.

The substrate contains no physical quantities. There is no mass, no energy, no charge, no field. There are only nodes, directed edges, and the connection relations among them.

The substrate is not "nothingness"—it is an underlying existence with definite topological structure and dynamics. Nodes exist, directed edges exist, and local reconnection events continuously occur within it. The substrate is an ever-restless topological sea, the raw material of all existence.

Definition 1.1: Loop
A closed path that starts from a node, proceeds along directed edges, and eventually returns to the starting node is called a loop. A loop is the only structure in the substrate that possesses "global identifiability."

Definition 1.2: Topological Charge
On any closed loop, the net directional algebraic sum along the loop traversal (forward edge count minus backward edge count) is an integer. This integer is conserved under reconnection operations—because the direction rule guarantees it.

This integer is called the topological charge of the loop.

Topological charge is not a presupposed physical quantity. It is the name given to the purely geometric fact of "the algebraic sum of directions on a closed path."

Postulate 2: Spontaneous Emergence of the Membrane
When the loop density (the number of closed paths contained per unit node) in a certain closure region of the substrate exceeds a critical value, the loops in that region, through collective reconnection, tend to weave into a closed surface. This surface divides the region into "inside" and "outside"—the inner loop density differs from the outer one, and the surface itself becomes the boundary between the two regions.

This closed surface is called the membrane.

The membrane is not "placed into" the substrate. It is a structural pattern that the substrate naturally tends toward under specific loop density conditions. The membrane is a state of the substrate—a phase transition product of the substrate from an unwoven state to a woven state. The moment of membrane formation establishes an inside-outside gradient: the interior is organized into a woven state, and the exterior remains in an unwoven state. Once this gradient is established, the membrane possesses topological persistence and can exist as an independent universe.

Definition 2.1 (Inside-Outside Distinction of the Membrane): Let membrane M be a connected subgraph of ℳ. Membrane M partitions ℳ into two disjoint sets of nodes—the interior is the set of nodes connected to M but not connected to infinity, and the exterior is the set of nodes connected to M and also connected to infinity. The emergence of the membrane is the phase transition moment at which ℳ first acquires this partitioning property.

Definition 2.2 (Ontological Status of the External Substrate): The external substrate and the internal substrate are ontologically the same entity—both are components of ℳ. Both consist of nodes and directed edges and both obey local reconnection dynamics. The difference lies only in the organizational mode: the interior of the membrane is in a woven state, possessing stable loops, shared link networks, and topological knots; the exterior of the membrane is in an unwoven state, with loop density below the critical value, unable to form a stable closed surface, and hence lacking shared link networks and topological knots. The external substrate is not nothingness—it has definite states, but its dynamical mode cannot directly couple with the interior of the membrane.

Postulate 3: Weaving Hierarchy
Material structures are formed by topological knots (indissoluble woven subgraphs) in a recursive hierarchy. Interactions between topological knots are realized through shared links. Every item on this recursive growth tree is a topological knot of a specific order, and its total number of primitive bricks is determined by the brick number of the preceding-generation knot.

Corollary 3.1: Hierarchical Truncation Criterion

The shared link network between topological knots is only counted as an effective interaction when the distance between the knots is less than or equal to a finite integer multiple of the recursive growth radius of the smaller knot.

Specifically, let the recursive order of topological knot A be m (determined by the number of primitive bricks N_A, in some packing configuration of N_A = 4^m), and its intrinsic combinatorial radius be r_A = ⌈N_A^{1/3}⌉ (in node steps).

When the center distance between two knots d_AB > κ·(r_A + r_B) (where κ is a screening factor determined by the local loop density, κ ∈ [1, 3]), the total number of shared links N_links decays exponentially to below a threshold N_thresh and can be regarded as zero in macroscopic physics.

The origin of this truncation: the intermediate loop density in the substrate at distances exceeding r_A + r_B cannot maintain stable weaving channels—any extra links will spontaneously dissolve through reconnection because intermediate nodes lack sufficient topological charge support.

Physical Consequences:

On the atomic nucleus scale (r ~ 1 fm): the truncation radius far exceeds the internucleon spacing, and the total number of shared links is geometrically forced to lock at maximum saturation—macroscopically manifesting as the short-range saturation of the strong nuclear force.

On the atomic scale (r ~ 1 Å): the truncation radius precisely matches the range of the electron cloud, and the total number of shared links naturally decreases with increasing distance according to the number of combinatorial paths—macroscopically manifesting as the 1/r form of the Coulomb potential.

On the cosmic scale (r ≳ 1 Mpc): the truncation radius matches the highest order of the recursive growth tree (galaxy cluster scale)—beyond this scale, the shared link count returns to zero.

Part II: Emergence of Space and Time
Postulate 4: Combinatorial Distance
The distance between two nodes is defined by the number of edges on the shortest path connecting them. The distance between two structures is defined by the number of edges on the shortest path connecting the regions to which they belong.

Distance is not a pre-existing metric in space. It is a direct reading of the connection relations between nodes.

Postulate 5: Space
The ensemble of all distance relations among structures on the membrane constitutes the spatial structure of the membrane.

Space is not a container that holds the membrane. It is the collection of relations among the structures on the membrane.

When a structure moves, it is not traveling through space—it is changing its connection relations with all other structures. "Motion" is the record of reconnection history in distance relations.

Postulate 6: Time
The sequential order between two events A and B on the membrane is determined by the cumulative number of reconnection events on the paths connecting them. If the total number of reconnections on any path from A to B is N, and N > 0, then A precedes B; if N = 0, then A and B are simultaneous.

Time is not a dimension, not a coordinate, not a parameter. It is the cumulative count of reconnection events.

Part III: Emergence of Material Structure
Postulate 7: Topological Knot
A woven subgraph on the membrane, composed of loops that do not spontaneously disintegrate under reconnection dynamics, is called a topological knot.

A subgraph does not spontaneously disintegrate if and only if: the number of consecutive reconnections required to separate it from the rest of the membrane exceeds a threshold. This threshold ensures that no local reconnection operation can release the internal stress without disrupting the overall structure.

Matter is not pre-existing particles. It is topological knots on the membrane that have reached stability conditions.

Definition 7.1: Stability Parameters of a Topological Knot

The stability of a topological knot is determined by two factors:

Total number of internal links: the number of independent connection paths among the loops within the knot. The larger the total number, the more reconnection steps are required to dismantle it.

Boundary connectivity: the number of connection paths between the knot surface and the exterior. The lower the boundary connectivity, the harder it is for external reconnections to invade the interior.

Definition 7.2: Topological Defects and Incomplete Surface Weaving

The surface weaving of any topological knot can never achieve absolutely perfect closure—there always exist regions that cannot be completely covered by the existing weaving. These are called topological defects. The size and distribution of defects depend on the weaving complexity of the knot: knots with fewer primitive bricks have loose weaving and larger defect area ratios; knots with more primitive bricks have dense weaving and smaller defect area ratios.

Topological defects are the "active sites" for interactions between topological knots. Whether two topological knots can undergo defect-complementary weaving depends entirely on the geometric matching degree of their surface defects.

Definition 7.2a (Scale Attribute of Topological Defects): Topological defects have only differences in scale size and distribution pattern. There is no absolutely defect-free complete topological knot. Light nuclei are loosely woven and have larger defect scales; heavy nuclei are tightly woven and have smaller defect scales. The defect scale monotonically decreases as nuclear mass increases but is never zero.

Definition 7.3: Primitive Brick

The smallest topological knot on the membrane that can exist independently and establish stable shared links with other topological knots is called a primitive brick.

The primitive brick is the effective basic unit at the current energy scale. Its "fundamentality" is energy-scale-dependent—if higher energy scales in the future reveal that the primitive brick is composed of smaller structures, the model can be naturally extended.

Definition 7.4: Inertia of a Primitive Brick

The ability of a primitive brick to resist directional reconnection—that is, the number of reconnection events required to change the net directional algebraic sum of its loops—is defined as the inertia of the primitive brick. In the phenomenal layer, this property is called "mass."

Postulate 8: Recursive Growth and the Spectrum of Topological Knots
Topological knots do not exist in isolation. Under appropriate energy scales and collision conditions, smaller stable topological knots can combine into larger stable topological knots through the defect-complementary weaving mechanism. When the defects of two smaller knots are exactly geometrically complementary, they mesh to form a larger woven structure—a process entirely determined by the geometric nature of the defects on both sides.

The defect distributions of smaller knots that can stably combine into a larger knot must satisfy specific compatibility conditions topologically. This naturally gives rise to a recursive growth tree of topological knots—starting from primitive bricks, combining generation by generation, forming stable knots with increasingly complex structures and greater inertia.

The Energy-Scale Truncation Rule for Recursive Growth

The recursive growth of topological knots does not proceed indefinitely. At a given loop density (corresponding to the energy scale or temperature in the phenomenal layer), only topological knots with inertia below a certain threshold can exist stably. When the recursive growth reaches the maximum stable weaving complexity at that energy scale, more complex topological knots will fail to form due to insufficient loop density support, or will be immediately torn apart by thermal fluctuations after formation.

At the current low energy scale of the universe (approximately 2.7 K cosmic microwave background), recursive growth is truncated at the level of baryon bricks (protons and neutrons). The internal structure of the primitive brick—if it exists—cannot be resolved at the current energy scale, so the primitive brick appears as an "elementary particle" in the phenomenal layer.

The essence of this truncation is the same as the Hierarchical Truncation Criterion (Corollary 3.1): when the weaving complexity exceeds the stability threshold of the shared link network that can be supported by the current loop density, higher-order topological knots cannot emerge.

Definition 8.1: Electron Brick

On the recursive growth tree, a stable topological knot formed by 4 primitive bricks in a specific weaving pattern is called an electron brick. It is the smallest independent stable knot at the current cosmic energy scale and is the basic building block for all more complex material structures.

The internal structure of the electron brick also follows the recursive weaving principle: it possesses a solid core tightly woven from those 4 primitive bricks, and possibly a soft outer layer composed of lower-level primitive bricks attached to the core through defects. At the current low energy scale of the universe, the inertia contribution of this outer layer is extremely tiny and can be neglected.

Definition 8.2: Exchange Reconnection Number and Geometric Enforcement of Pauli Exclusion

Definition 8.2a (Exchange Reconnection Number 𝒮): Let p_A, p_B be two dangling link ports, one on the surface of each of two identical topological knots (electron bricks). The minimum number of local reconnection operation steps required to move p_A to the original position of p_B and p_B to the original position of p_A is called the exchange reconnection number 𝒮.

Lemma 8.1 (Parity of Exchange): For two identical topological knots occupying the same order on the recursive growth tree, the exchange reconnection number 𝒮 of their ports is necessarily odd.

Sketch of proof: The exchange operation of two identical topological knots must be accomplished by a braiding exchange (going around a closed loop, rather than dragging nodes directly through each other). Each local reconnection operation changes the target node of one directed edge. For a complete braiding exchange of two identical objects, the length k of the exchange generator sequence of the braid group B_N must satisfy k ≡ 1 (mod 2). The simplest constructive example requires 3 local reconnections. Hence, 𝒮 must be odd.

Theorem 8.1 (Geometric Enforcement of Pauli Exclusion): Two identical electron bricks cannot occupy exactly the same geometric position and phase state.

Proof: If two identical electron bricks occupy exactly the same geometric state, the exchange operation degenerates into the identity operation (0 reconnection steps, even). But Lemma 8.1 requires the exchange reconnection number to be odd. The contradiction that an odd number equals an even number forces the assumption to be false. The two identical electron bricks must maintain a difference in at least one geometric degree of freedom—either a phase difference Δθ ≥ 1, or they occupy different spatial winding mode numbers.

Definition 8.3: Baryon Brick and the Dual-Layer Structure

At orders far higher than the electron brick on the recursive growth tree, there exist giant stable topological knots woven from a large number of electron bricks and lower-level primitive bricks according to recursive rules.

The structure of such topological knots exhibits a universal dual-layer mode:

Solid Core: Composed of a large number of electron bricks or equivalent primitive bricks in the closest packing arrangement. For macroscopic-scale topological knots, cubic close-packing is the optimal arrangement, and the number of core primitive bricks approaches an integer multiple of 12³ (1728) in this limit. The core provides a high binding energy to resist disintegration.

Soft Outer Layer: Composed of a large number of primitive bricks or small topological knots floating on the core surface, attached to the core through defect complementarity, forming a deformable buffer layer. The outer layer bricks can slide under external force, absorbing collision energy. The defect distribution of the outer layer bricks determines the macroscopic charge manifestation of the topological knot.

These two stable configurations correspond respectively to the proton (positively charged) and the neutron (electrically neutral) in the phenomenal layer. Both share the same structural pattern, differing only in the net topological charge attribute of the outer layer defects.

Definition 8.4: Total Compressive Binding Energy (E_comp)

As a closed woven network, a topological knot has a structural threshold to resist external compression. This threshold, jointly determined by the strength of all connecting edges within the knot, the weaving configuration, and the defect distribution, is called the total compressive binding energy, denoted E_comp.

E_comp is a single unified physical quantity for resisting compression of the topological knot, without presupposing its internal microscopic decomposition structure. Its essence is the structural resistance of the topological knot as a closed woven network to maintain its configuration.

Definition 8.4a: Hierarchical Compressive Tensor

The compressive binding energy ℰ_comp of an atomic nucleus is decomposed into three geometrically independent contribution terms:

E_comp = 𝒱_core · 𝒮_core + 𝒱_shell · 𝒮_shell + ℐ(suture)

where:

𝒱_core: the number of close-packed primitive brick units in the core, determining the mass number bulk of the nucleus

𝒮_core: the average link saturation of the core. For perfect close-packing (e.g., the 12³ hypercube closure), 𝒮_core → 1

𝒱_shell: the number of effective primitive brick units in the outer shell participating in chemical bonding

𝒮_shell: the port saturation of the outer shell, determining the chemical valence state and reaction activity

ℐ(suture): the topological stress concentration factor on the core-shell transition suture line, determined by the deviation of the suture layer brick arrangement from the ideal closure configuration

Theorem 8.2: Chemical-Nuclear Decoupling Theorem

The spatial orientation of the dangling ports on the outer shell (i.e., the chemical valence state) depends only on 𝒮_shell and the arrangement of the outer shell ports, and is independent of the total number of core primitive bricks 𝒱_core. The suture line stress concentration factor ℐ, however, is highly sensitive to discrete changes in the core brick number.

Corollary 8.1 (Vacancy-Induced Stress Concentration): If the core brick arrangement has vacancies (i.e., the actual number of bricks is less than the ideal closure number for that weaving tree order), the topological stress concentration factor on the suture line ℐ > 0. If there are no vacancies, ℐ ≈ 0.

Corollary 8.2 (Fission Threshold Differences of Uranium Isotopes):

²³⁸U has its core brick arrangement matching the complete closure of the 12³ hypercube, ℐ_238 ≈ 0, and a high fission trigger threshold.

²³⁵U lacks 3 brick units in its core, and the vacancies cause ℐ_235 > 0, making its fission trigger threshold significantly lower than that of ²³⁸U.

The chemical properties (𝒮_shell) of both are completely identical—the outer shell port arrangement is unaffected by the core vacancies.

Part IV: Nuclear Transformations—The Four Reaction Channels
4.1 Matter: Stable Topological Knots and Topological Defects
Matter particles are stable topological knots on the membrane. Every topological knot carries regions that weaving cannot completely close—topological defects. Defects differ only in scale size and distribution pattern; there is no absolutely defect-free complete topological knot. Light nuclei are loosely woven with larger defect scales; heavy nuclei are tightly woven with smaller defect scales.

4.2 General Reaction Formula and the Four Mechanisms
The interaction between topological knots is essentially the complementary matching of their surface defects and the restructuring of connecting edges. The complete general formula for a nuclear reaction is written as:

a + b → x → c + d

The forward polymerization (a + b → x) is driven by Path I or Path II. After the intermediate nucleus x is formed, its subsequent fate is taken over by one of three paths: if the fission threshold of x is exceeded, it fissions into c and d; if the global topological charges of a and b happen to exactly cancel, Path III directly takes over, skipping x. The four mechanisms are described below.

Channel I: Defect Complementary Weaving (Path I)

The essential characteristic of Path I is defect-induced, stepwise reconnection.

Condition: The topological defects on the surfaces of two topological knots are exactly geometrically complementary.

Process: The defect regions of the two knots mesh, and the connecting edges are restructured stepwise and orderly, forming a new stable topological knot x. The energy released in the reaction depends on the total inertia deficit of the participating topological knots.

Characteristics:

The polymerization probability of Path I depends on the geometric matching degree of the surface defects of the two topological knots. As nuclear mass increases, weaving tends to become denser, and the defect area ratio monotonically decreases—hence, in qualitative trend, the polymerization probability decreases with increasing mass. But this decrease is not to zero; there is a non-zero polymerization probability between any two topological knots.

In an environment with sufficient heat dissipation time and thermal expansion space, Path I can form a negative-feedback self-stabilizing loop: local temperature rise induces expansion, defect spacing increases, matching probability drops, and heat release automatically decreases. The gentle burning lasting billions of years inside stars is the operation of this mechanism.

Negative feedback is not automatically obtained but is a product of specific environmental conditions. It requires the medium to have rapid heat dissipation capability (such as fluid convection) and immediate thermal expansion response (density rapidly decreases as temperature rises). Gaseous and high-temperature molten celestial bodies satisfy these two conditions, so Path I can operate stably and continuously inside them. Solid celestial bodies lack both—but if the interior temperature of a solid body rises to melting, and the medium acquires fluid properties, negative feedback can be established.

The polymerization route (stepwise or checker-jump) is dynamically determined by the raw material abundance ratio.

Channel II: Forced Chain Reconnection (Path II)

The essential characteristic of Path II is batch cutting and collective restructuring—it does not depend on defect matching probability, does not pick matching partners, and once triggered, inevitably proceeds and cannot be regulated by environmental feedback.

Condition: The number of local reconnection operations exceeds the batch breaking threshold of connecting edges, i.e., the external compression energy exceeds the total compressive binding energy E_comp of the topological knot.

Process: Connecting edges are batch-cut, the core weaving network is exposed, and large-scale chain forced reconnection occurs. In a single Path II event, a large number of topological knots are simultaneously dismantled and reconnected in an extremely short time, macroscopically manifesting as an instantaneous, violent release of energy.

Characteristics:

The final state of Path II is still topological knots (fragment nuclei c and d).

The trigger condition is universal: the source of external compression energy is not limited to gravity—any form of extreme compression/heating such as laser fusion, magnetic confinement, shock waves, as long as the injected energy exceeds the E_comp of the nucleus, can forcibly trigger Path II.

The core dismantling in supernova explosions falls into this category.

Channel III: Topological Dissolution (Path III)

Condition: The algebraic sum of the global topological charges of the two topological knots a and b is exactly zero.

Process: The defect complementary meshing does not form a new stable weave—at the moment of meshing, the entire topological configuration undergoes complete dissolution due to the global stress returning to zero. All connecting edges are simultaneously dismantled, and all topological nodes return to the planar membrane with zero topological charge (network ground state). The final state is only pure radiation, containing no topological knots. The energy released is equivalent to the total inertia of the participating topological knots, with a conversion efficiency of 100%.

Characteristics:

In the reaction formula a + b → x → c + d, Path III corresponds to a + b → pure radiation, completely skipping the intermediate nucleus x.

Path III shares the trigger condition of "defect complementary meshing" with Path I, differing in the outcome of the meshing. The probability of Path III depends on the precision of topological charge pairing and is extremely rare under natural conditions.

Path III only blocks a specific reaction channel (a + b → pure radiation) and does not mean that a nuclide of that mass number cannot be synthesized through other precursor combinations.

Channel IV: Stress Tearing (Fission)

Condition: After the intermediate nucleus x has been formed via Path I or Path II, the accumulated stress on a certain internal suture line exceeds the instantaneous bearing limit of the connecting edges. The internal structure of the nuclide is not homogeneous—different suture lines bear different tensile stresses on their connecting edges.

Process: The configuration undergoes mechanical tearing along that suture line, splitting into two smaller nuclei c and d. The energy released equals the reduction in total inertia before and after tearing.

Characteristics:

Fission probability depends on the stress distribution of each suture line inside the nuclide. The more concentrated the stress on a suture line and the closer it is to the bearing limit of connecting edges, the higher the fission probability of that nuclide.

Fission probability is one of the three key factors determining whether a nuclide can persist long-term in nature (see the Three-Factor Criterion in Part V).

4.3 Summary of the Complete Loop
In the reaction formula a + b → x → c + d, the complete loop is:

a + b → x: Path I (defect-induced, stepwise reconnection) or Path II (batch cutting, collective restructuring);

x → c + d: Fission (stress tearing), with products returning to the raw material pool;

a + b → pure radiation: Path III (topological dissolution), skipping x, directly returning to zero.

4.4 The Deep Mechanism of the Coulomb Barrier
Whether two topological knots can mesh depends on the configuration matching degree of their surface defects. An increase in temperature means larger thermal vibration amplitudes, more fully exposed defect sites, and a higher matching probability. The Coulomb barrier, as measured macroscopically, is the statistical failure rate of countless defect matching attempts. It is not an independent force, but the macroscopic statistical manifestation of probability.

Part V: Elemental Abundance and the Iron Peak—The Three-Factor Criterion
5.1 The Three-Factor Criterion
In the reaction network composed of the four channels, the abundance of any nuclide in the universe is jointly determined by the following three probabilities:

Synthesis Probability: The probability that the nuclide is manufactured through various precursor channels. If a channel is blocked by Path III, its contribution is zero, but the nuclide can still be synthesized through other channels.

Continued Polymerization Probability: The probability that the nuclide, acting as a reactant, continues to polymerize into heavier nuclides through Path I or Path II.

Fission Probability: The probability that the nuclide fissions back into lighter nuclei due to internal stress tearing.

These three jointly determine the net retention of the nuclide on the mass spectrum:

Net Retention ∝ Synthesis Probability × (1 - Continued Polymerization Probability - Fission Probability)

Abundance peaks appear at nuclides where "the synthesis probability is relatively considerable, but both the continued polymerization probability and the fission probability are low"—the nucleon flux can reach here, but after arriving, it is neither easy to continue moving forward nor easy to be pushed back, hence net accumulation. Abundance valleys appear at nuclides where "synthesis probability is low" or "continued polymerization probability is high" or "fission probability is high"—the nucleon flux either cannot arrive, or cannot stay if it does.

The entire abundance distribution of the periodic table is the comprehensive projection of these three probabilities continuously varying across the mass spectrum.

5.2 Checker-Jump Path and Stepwise Path
Under this framework, there is no "necessary route" on the nuclide mass spectrum. Any two topological knots (light, medium, or heavy) can polymerize as long as their defects match. The reaction probability depends on the geometric matching degree of the defects on both sides and has no rigid constraint with their position on the mass spectrum.

The checker-jump path and the stepwise path are not mutually exclusive but are two parallel channels whose weights are determined by the raw material abundance ratio. When the matter abundance was uniform in the early universe, the checker-jump path became the dominant channel and could exponentially accelerate structural growth.

5.3 The Iron Peak: Concrete Manifestation of the Three-Factor Criterion
The prominent abundance of iron-56 is the concrete manifestation of the above three-factor criterion at mass number 56:

Synthesis Probability: The nucleon flux can reach mass number 56 through both the stepwise path and the checker-jump path.

Continued Polymerization Probability: The topological knot at mass number 56 has an extremely small defect area ratio, so the probability of continuing to polymerize upward as a reactant is relatively low—the nucleon flux "moving forward" from here is slow.

Fission Probability: The topological knot at mass number 56 has balanced internal suture line stresses, so the probability of being torn back into lighter nuclei is also low—the nucleon flux "being pushed back" from here is also slow.

The superposition of the three: the nucleon flux can reach mass number 56, but after arriving, the rates of moving forward and flowing backward are both low—net accumulation. This is the complete cause of the iron peak. As to which specific isotope form the topological knot of mass number 56 persists in, that is determined by the specific fission probability of that isotope and does not affect the fact that iron peak is an abundance peak at that mass number.

The iron peak has no causal relationship with stellar evolution. Iron is prominent in abundance only because the nucleon flux has a large net accumulation there. It does not determine any evolutionary turning point of a star. A star can trigger Path II at any stage—regardless of which mass number happens to form an abundance peak on the mass spectrum.

5.4 The Astrophysical Role of the Iron Core
Abundance Aspect: The position of the iron peak is determined by the three-factor criterion.

Structural Aspect: The special role of the iron core in stellar evolution stems from its extremely high total compressive binding energy E_comp. The Path I polymerization probability of iron nuclei is extremely low, but E_comp is extremely high, so the iron core can withstand very high gravitational compression energy without triggering Path II. When the peripheral material continues to accumulate and the gravitational compression energy exceeds the E_comp threshold of the iron core, Path II is triggered.

5.5 Examples of Checker-Jump Reactions
The following reaction formulas only indicate mass number polymerization and do not pre-specify which specific isotope the product persists as. Whether the product is a stable nuclide, an unstable isotope, or fissions immediately after fusion is determined by the fission probability of the topological knot of that mass number.

Two carbon nuclei (A=12+12) → topological knot of mass number 24

Two oxygen nuclei (A=16+16) → topological knot of mass number 32

Carbon + oxygen (A=12+16) → topological knot of mass number 28

Oxygen + neon (A=16+20) → topological knot of mass number 36

Two helium nuclei (A=4+4) → topological knot of mass number 8 (high fission probability, fissions back immediately, or continues to capture helium → topological knot of mass number 12)

5.6 Geometric Inevitability of Light Element Enrichment and Its Geological Consequences
The polymerization probability of Path I depends on the geometric matching degree of the surface defects of the two topological knots. Light nuclei (carbon, nitrogen, oxygen, etc.) are loosely woven and have relatively large defect area ratios, so their matching probability for participating in Path I polymerization is qualitatively higher than that of heavy nuclei. Meanwhile, the particle number base of light nuclei in the initial material of the solar system is far larger than that of heavy nuclei. The superposition of the two—high matching probability and large number base—makes the total synthetic yield of light nuclides in the Path I polymerization-fission cycle far greater than that of heavy nuclides. This is the geometric root of the relatively high cosmic abundance of life-essential elements such as carbon and oxygen, involving no special mechanisms.

Inside the Earth, carbon and hydrogen continuously synthesized by Path I in the core upwell to the crust through mantle plumes and deep fluid channels. This massive supply of light nuclides provides the material precondition for the abiogenic chemical synthesis of hydrocarbons in the crust. TDMU itself does not describe chemical bonding processes but offers a geometric explanation at the nucleosynthesis level for the deep inorganic origin of hydrocarbons. This is qualitatively compatible with observations that certain ultra-deep oil reservoirs lack biomarkers and are associated with mantle-derived helium (high ³He/⁴He).

5.7 Checker-Jump Synthesis of Heavy Nuclei Inside Planets (Applied Corollary)
The checker-jump path allows any two topological knots to polymerize directly without stepwise climbing. Under the conditions of the Earth's outer core (approximately 10⁵⁰ nuclides, high pressure 135–360 GPa, temperature 4000–6000 K, integrated over 4.5 billion years), two medium-heavy nuclei can fuse via a checker-jump to form an intermediate nucleus of mass number ≥197, which then sheds a light fragment to stabilize at the 197 channel; the absolute event count can reach an appreciable level. The total amount of gold synthesized in the Earth's core is estimated at about 10¹¹ tons—the vast majority remains in the core, and only a very tiny amount enters the crust through core-mantle boundary exchange (observed crustal reserve ~2×10⁹ tons), with both quantities being mutually consistent in order of magnitude.

Part VI: Interactions—The Geometry of Shared Links
Postulate 9: Shared Links
The multiple connection paths established between two topological knots through intermediate loops on the membrane are called shared links. Shared links are the sole medium of interaction between topological knots.

There is no independent "force" entity. All interactions are manifestations of the geometric properties of shared links.

Definition 9.1: Five Geometric Parameters of Shared Links
All interactions can be reduced to the following five geometric parameters of shared links:

Parameter	Definition	Phenomenal Layer Manifestation
Total Link Number	The number of independent connection paths between two topological knots	Strength of gravity
Link Orientation Degree	The statistical uniformity of shared links in spatial directions	Strength of magnetism
Link Density Gradient	The rate of change of link number in space	Strength of electricity
Link Saturation	The proportion of links to all possible connection paths	Strong nuclear force (attraction/repulsion)
Link Fragility	The probability of breaking under perturbation	Weak force decay rate
6.1 Distinction of Geometric Degrees of Freedom between Light and Gravity
The shared link network possesses three independent geometric degrees of freedom:

Degree of Freedom I: Total Link Number N_total (static/quasi-static scalar count)
Determines gravity. This total number is unaffected by density gradient potential wells. Even if radially propagating waves are completely trapped, the restoring stress corresponding to the total number still spans enormous radii and acts on external matter. Physical correspondence: gravitational work, rigid torque transfer.

Degree of Freedom II: Density Gradient ∇ρ_link and Orientation Wave δ𝒜 (dynamic transverse wave perturbation)
Determines light (electromagnetic radiation in the phenomenal layer). First-order/second-order gradient potential wells only act on these transverse waves. They do not act on the static tension count. Physical correspondence: normal light radiation, photon capture.

Degree of Freedom III: Density Fluctuation Longitudinal Wave δρ_link
Longitudinal compression-tension stress waves of the shared link density (phonon mode in the phenomenal layer). Optical potential wells only act on the transverse wave δ𝒜; longitudinal waves are not constrained by such potential wells.

Core Conclusion (Gravity-Light Decoupling): Gravity (N_total) and light (δ𝒜) are carried by completely independent geometric degrees of freedom. The capture of light does not mean gravity is isolated; the penetration of gravity through a capture surface does not mean light can escape.

6.1.1 Further Elaboration on the Ontology of Light
Light's ontological status in TDMU is fundamentally different from that in the Standard Model. Light is not an independently existing particle, nor an entity of "wave-particle duality." Light is the dynamic transverse wave perturbation of the orientation of shared links (δ𝒜).

Specifically:

Carrier Medium: The propagation of light requires a medium, namely the shared link network pervading the cosmic membrane. Regions without a shared link network (such as the external substrate outside the membrane) do not contain light.

Content of Vibration: What vibrates is not an electric or magnetic field, but the spatial orientation arrangement of the shared links. When shared links exhibit statistically non-uniform distribution in spatial directions, magnetism (a static torsional stress field) is generated; when this non-uniformity propagates along the shared link network in the form of a transverse wave, it manifests as light.

Photon: There is no independent photon particle. A "photon" is a local excitation of the discrete phase field Θ(r_i)—a ripple on the shared link network. Its "particle nature" originates from the discretization constraints on perturbation transmission imposed by the woven network.

Speed of Light: The speed of light c is an intrinsic property of the shared link network. It is determined by the local reconnection dynamics of the substrate—the shared link network has a characteristic response time, the minimum number of local reconnection operation steps required for a node to transmit a perturbation to the next node after receiving it. The speed of light equals the node spacing (minimum link step length) multiplied by the upper limit of the reconnection rate. The uniformity of the speed of light on the membrane stems from the uniformity of the elasticity and inertia of the shared link medium itself at all free-space locations. The constancy of the speed of light is not a presupposed property of spacetime geometry but an emergent result of the uniformity of the shared link medium.

6.2 Construction of the Discrete Gauge Field
Definition 9.2 (Combinatorial Covariant Derivative): Let the discrete phase field of shared links be Θ(r_i), defined on lattice node i. Let 𝒜_ij be the orientation tensor contribution of the background shared links between nodes i, j. Define the combinatorial covariant derivative:

D_ij Θ ≡ Θ(r_j) - Θ(r_i) - 𝒜_ij

where:

Θ(r_i) ∈ ℤ is the phase of shared links at node i (reconnection step count)

𝒜_ij is the background orientation field between nodes i and j generated by the periodic arrangement of atomic nuclei, defined as the minimum number of reconnection steps to go along the direction i → j

All quantities are purely discrete and independent of continuous spatial metrics.

6.3 Geometric Definition of the Superconducting State
Definition 9.3 (Superconducting State): If and only if for all nearest-neighbor node pairs ⟨i, j⟩, the covariant derivative vanishes:

D_ij Θ = 0, ∀⟨i, j⟩ ∈ conductive network

the system is in a globally phase-locked state—the helical motions of all shared links are perfectly synchronized.

6.4 Discrete Derivation of the First London Equation
In TDMU, the phenomenal electric current density J is defined as the directed shared link flux. When the system is in a state that deviates from perfect superconductivity but still maintains phase coherence, the covariant derivative has a residual value. This residual value drives the directed link flux:

J ∝ Σ_{⟨i,j⟩} D_ij Θ

In the continuum limit, D_ij Θ corresponds to the covariant derivative ∇Θ - A. Under the London gauge:

J = - (1/Λ) A

where Λ is the geometric inertia constant of the shared link network, determined by the total link number per unit volume N_total: Λ ∝ 1/N_total. This is the purely discrete form of the first London equation.

6.5 Geometric Inevitability of the Meissner Effect
Taking the discrete curl of the London equation:

∇ × J = - (1/Λ) (∇ × A) = - (1/Λ) B

Inside the superconducting state, all D_ij Θ = 0, so the spatial distribution of J is completely determined by the configuration of 𝒜_ij, and ∇ × J = 0.

From this, B = 0 strictly holds inside the superconductor. The penetration depth λ_L = √Λ is defined as the geometric yielding step number of the shared link network.

6.6 Topological Inevitability of Flux Quantization
Consider a closed path C on a superconducting ring. The phase Θ must return to the same discrete count after one round, so the total phase change is an integer multiple of 2π.

Summing the covariant derivative along the closed path:

∮_C DΘ · dl = 2π n, n ∈ ℤ

In the superconducting state, DΘ = 0 holds inside the ring body, but along a closed path that encloses the ring hole, the contribution of A is not canceled. Hence:

∮_C DΘ · dl = ∮_C A · dl = Φ

Combining, we obtain:

Φ = n · Φ₀, Φ₀ = 2π

The magnetic flux is rigorously quantized in the geometric natural units of TDMU as integer multiples of the elementary unit Φ₀ = 2π.

6.7 Geometric Definition of the Five Fundamental Interactions
Definition 9.4: Gravity
The larger the total number of shared links between two topological knots, the stronger the tendency of the membrane to reduce overall stress—this tendency manifests in the phenomenal layer as pulling the two knots toward each other. Gravity is the large-number statistical effect of the total shared link count. No graviton is needed.

Definition 9.5: Magnetism
When shared links show a statistically non-uniform distribution in spatial directions, a macroscopic torsional stress field is generated. The streamlines of this stress field are the magnetic field lines. The closed nature of magnetic field lines is a topological necessity—shared links cannot have isolated endpoints in space. Magnetic monopoles are topologically forbidden.

Definition 9.6: Electricity
The density gradient of shared links generates normal stress waves. There exists a stress difference between regions of density surplus and regions of density deficit:

Surplus-surplus (or deficit-deficit) → stress difference is repulsion

Surplus-deficit → stress difference is attraction
Electricity is the orthogonal deformation mode of magnetism—the two together constitute the complete linear response of shared link perturbations.

Definition 9.7: Strong Nuclear Force
When the distance between two topological knots shrinks to near the maximum saturation of shared links:

Shared links form an inseparable shared network → dismantling requires simultaneously cutting a large number of links → attraction

If the distance further shrinks, the shared link paths are forced to bend → produces repulsive bow stress → repulsion
The strong nuclear force is the nonlinear behavior mode of shared links at extremely short distances.

Definition 9.8: Weak Interaction
When the distance between two topological knots is in the critical range where shared links "can just be established," the number of shared links is very small. This few-link state is metastable—any local reconnection perturbation can cause link breakage and trigger a cascade disintegration. The weak interaction is not a "force" but the spontaneous decay mode of a metastable shared link network.

Definition 9.9: Phenomenal Electric Current
In a conductor, the shared links of topological knots on the surface of atomic nuclei are distributed along closed paths in the absence of an external field—this is the lowest energy mode of shared links in cylindrical geometry. An external field (density gradient of shared links) stretches these closed paths into helical paths along the conductor direction. The helically advancing shared links drag the peripheral electron bricks to move synchronously. Electric current is the collective manifestation of the helical motion of shared links. Resistance is the statistical frequency of collisions and reconnections of shared links during motion.

Definition 9.10: Superconductivity
When the helical motion of shared links achieves complete synchronization—all links have consistent phase and the collision frequency drops to zero—shared links enter a dissipationless transmission state. Superconductivity is the synchronized phase transition of the collective motion mode of shared links.

Part VII: Life Cycle of the Universe and the Spectrum of Astrophysical Evolution
Postulate 10: The Membrane Emergence Event
In a region of the substrate, the loop density exceeds a critical value due to intrinsic fluctuations. A large number of forward and backward direction loops form paired structures, squeezed by peripheral circulating flows until compressed beyond their elastic recovery point. At the critical point, the system becomes unstable, and the topological elastic potential energy drives the loop surfaces to evert outward and form a closed membrane.

In the phenomenal layer, this event is observed as the "beginning" of the universe, but in the underlying framework, there is no time zero point—membrane emergence is a phase transition process of the substrate from one topological state to another.

Intuitive dynamical picture: a pot of unbounded, thick liquid substrate, reaching a critical phase transition threshold due to intrinsic statistical fluctuations, giving birth to a topological bubble that expands and eventually dissipates. This is the membrane emergence event of the universe.

Postulate 11: Evolution
After formation, the membrane continues to expand. Expansion leads to an overall decrease in the loop density on the membrane—phenomenally called "cooling." Temperature is the local manifestation of the loop density gradient.

During the cooling process, topological knots emerge sequentially: primitive bricks → electron bricks → leptons → mesons → baryons → atomic nuclei → atoms → molecules.

Each step is the most stable woven structure "allowed to exist" at the current loop density. As the loop density continues to drop, recursive growth is truncated at the level of baryon bricks—higher-order topological knots cannot exist stably due to lack of sufficient loop density support (see "Energy-Scale Truncation Rule for Recursive Growth" in Part III).

The current membrane is in an intermediate state of "loop density low enough but not yet low enough for membrane dissolution."

Postulate 12: The Finale
The membrane is always a part of the substrate. The circulating pressure in the region outside the membrane does not attenuate with membrane expansion. When the loop density on the membrane falls below a critical value, the compressive strength of the membrane will be lower than the external circulating pressure, and the membrane will enter irreversible structural instability.

Final outcome: global topological reconnection dissolution—the membrane shatters into discrete loops and returns to the substrate.

The finality of global membrane dissolution refers only to the end of the membrane's status as a coherent topological entity, not an absolute prohibition of defect-complementary weaving among local topological knots. Although the Path I event rate R of the woven solidified remnant is perpetually positive, its coherence timescale far exceeds the Hubble time due to the exponential increase in topological knot spacing.

7.1 The Continuous Spectrum of Celestial Bodies
In TDMU, astrophysical evolution is refined into a dynamical game among Path I, Path II, Fission, and Path III at different scales. All celestial bodies follow a single fundamental law in the phenomenal layer: total inertia reduction is converted into stress-wave energy release.

7.1.1 Solid Planets: The Cutoff Condition

Solid media have two fatal weaknesses: extremely slow heat dissipation (heat is conducted by lattice vibrations, far less efficient than fluid convection), and severely limited thermal expansion (constrained by the crystal lattice, local temperature rise does not immediately lead to density decrease).

Once the Path I reaction rate in the core of a solid planet enters positive feedback, the solid medium neither dissipates heat in time nor expands in time to lower the reaction rate. Local stress soars exponentially and breaches the connecting edge batch fracture threshold in an extremely short time. Path II is detonated, and the entire planet is dismantled layer by layer outward from the core.

Critical Condition: Solid planets have a critical upper limit of inertia. For planets exceeding this inertia, regardless of initial phenomenal temperature, the thermal runaway speed of core Path I will exceed the lattice heat conduction speed, triggering irreversible positive feedback until Path II is detonated.

Survival Condition: If the inertia of a solid planet is below the critical value, or if internal radioactive decay/tidal heating is sufficient to heat the core to a molten state, the medium acquires fluid properties—convective heat dissipation and thermal expansion response begin to function. The negative feedback mechanism of Path I can be established, and the star may exist stably.

7.1.2 Gaseous Planets: The Smoldering Topological Furnace

Fluid media dissipate heat quickly, expand when heated, and density drops, automatically down-regulating the reaction rate. From mini-Neptunes to hot Jupiters to brown dwarfs, gaseous planets exist stably over a wide range of inertia.

Phenomenal layer observations: Jupiter's net internal heat flux is about 5.5×10¹⁷ W, and Saturn's internal heat flux is 2.84±0.20 W/m². Path I offers a unified explanation: both Jupiter and Saturn run Path I defect-complementary weaving reactions in their cores, differing only in the macroscopic event rate.

7.1.3 The Spectrum of Topological Furnaces

The Sun, Jupiter, and Saturn in the phenomenal layer form a complete spectrum from "burning" to "smoldering." Planets are not "failed stars"—they are topological furnaces where gravity has pressed the nuclear fire to just smoldering, rather than completely extinguishing it.

7.1.4 Main-Sequence Stars: The Core of the Burning Path

When the inertia of a gaseous clump exceeds a certain critical threshold, the defect matching probability in the core breaks through a key threshold—the defect complementary matching probability among light nuclear topological knots rises to a level sufficient to support the luminosity of the entire clump.

The essence of a main-sequence star: dominated by Path I, under the conditions of rapid heat dissipation and immediate thermal expansion feedback provided by the fluid medium, it reaches a dynamic equilibrium with the environmental thermodynamic response.

Checker-jump nuclear burning: the nucleon flux in the stellar core is not a one-way street. The checker-jump path allows polymerization between any two nuclides. Light-nucleus fusion dominates the luminosity, and medium-heavy nucleus fusion proceeds in parallel at a lower rate.

7.2 Accretion-Dissipation Cycle and the Complete Spectrum of Astrophysical Evolution
The fate of a star is not determined by a unidirectional sequence of "nuclear fuel exhaustion → inevitable collapse" but by a dynamical game jointly determined by total accreted mass, accretion rate, envelope dissipation rate, and core vortex decoherence rate. The evolutionary starting point is the gravitational condensation of nebular gas under the shared-link convergence effect. Depending on the total accreted mass and environmental conditions, the condensed body differentiates into three initial paths, and migration among the three paths can occur during the star's lifetime due to changes in the external matter supply rate.

Initial Differentiation—Three Paths:

Path A (Smoldering Path): Insufficient accreted mass. After gravitational condensation, the core defect matching probability remains consistently below the stable burning threshold. Path I operates only at a very low event rate. The star does not emit light, only producing infrared thermal radiation. This path corresponds to brown dwarfs and gas giant planets (e.g., Jupiter, Saturn). They can exist stably for long periods, with cores continuously smoldering for billions of years.

Path B (Burning Path): Moderate accreted mass. After gravitational condensation and continuous accretion, the core loop density breaks through the critical point, and Path I enters negative-feedback equilibrium burning. The star stably emits light for billions of years. This path corresponds to main-sequence stars (e.g., the Sun). Main-sequence stars are the natural result of moderate accretion, not a special branch. Their stable burning is built on the rapid convective heat dissipation and immediate thermal expansion feedback provided by the fluid medium (plasma).

Path C (Camouflage Path): Oversaturated accreted mass. After gravitational condensation and extremely rapid accretion, the loop density in the central region far exceeds the critical point, and a black-hole-class topological star core forms first. External matter piles up around the core into a thick, optically thick envelope. Path I dominates the surface layer of the envelope, self-stabilizing and emitting light through negative feedback. Observers can only see the thermal radiation of the envelope and cannot detect the black-hole-class topological star core inside. This path corresponds to extremely massive stars (e.g., R136a1, ~315 M_⊙) and extremely large volume stars (e.g., Stephenson 2-18, ~2150 R_⊙). The former is the case of a dense, high-temperature envelope; the latter is the case of a tenuous, low-temperature envelope.

The three paths are not strictly isolated. During the star's lifetime, changes in the external matter supply rate can cause the star to migrate among different paths. A main-sequence star (Path B) can enter the camouflage path (Path C) after a sudden increase in accretion rate; a camouflage-state star can re-expose itself as a normal main-sequence star after envelope dissipation, or even degenerate into a smoldering-state body.

Common Subsequent Evolution—Envelope Stage:

Regardless of the initial path, as long as the star possesses an envelope, it enters the envelope evolution stage. The envelope is formed by the accumulation of accreted material, with the surface layer dominated by light nuclei, and Path I defect-complementary weaving is predominant. Because the envelope is a fluid medium, Path I has negative-feedback self-stabilizing ability: local heat release → expansion → density decrease → defect spacing increase → matching probability decrease → heat release automatically reduces → cooling contraction → density recovers → matching probability recovers.

A branch event may be triggered at this stage: local Path II eruption. When the accretion compression rate exceeds the E_comp of local nuclides in the envelope, Path II is triggered inside the envelope. The gravitational binding of the envelope material is relatively shallow, so the eruption is usually limited to flares, pulsations, or localized mass ejections, without destroying the core structure. Observational correspondences: stellar flares, non-radial pulsations, certain novae or dwarf nova eruptions.

When the external matter supply declines, the envelope gradually dissipates, Path I burning weakens, and luminosity drops. After the envelope is completely exhausted, the core is exposed. The nature of the core depends on the initial path and its evolutionary history:

If the core mass is below the black hole critical point (from low-mass main-sequence stars in Path B), it is exposed as a degenerate thermal remnant—a white dwarf. The Path I in the white dwarf core is nearly frozen because the density is insufficient to sustain high-frequency collisions. It only glows by residual heat and slowly cools. If the white dwarf is in a binary system and accretes material from a companion star, causing its mass to grow close to the critical point, Path I is reactivated, locally triggering Path II. Moreover, because the degenerate state lacks negative feedback, the entire star instantaneously undergoes a chain reconnection—a Type Ia supernova, completely disintegrating with no remnant.

If the core mass is above the black hole critical point (from Path C, or from a high-mass main-sequence star in Path B that evolves until the core density breaks through the critical point), it is exposed as a superfluid vortex condensate—a black-hole-class topological star (a "black hole" in the phenomenal layer). It now enters the black hole exposed stage, with the observational correspondence being a hungry bare core (e.g., Sgr A*). If external matter is supplied again, the star can return to the envelope evolution stage, forming a cycle.

Common Subsequent Evolution—Decoherence Stage:

After the black hole is exposed, it faces two possible fates.

Mainstream Path—Silent Decoherence: The vortex twist energy continuously and slowly leaks away through the background longitudinal waves. The superfluid state smoothly melts on an extremely long timescale, and the black hole mass gradually decreases. When the mass drops to a critical value at which it can no longer maintain the global phase-locking condition D_ijΘ = 0, the superfluid state collapses, and the vortex decoheres—the frozen vortex twist energy is released, and the woven network solidifies. The star enters the woven solidified state—a rigid solid network with only Path I operating at an extremely low event rate. In this stage, the solid medium lacks fluid negative feedback, and the stress accumulated by Path I periodically triggers localized Path II, generating pulsed radiation. Observational correspondence: pulsars, neutron stars. If Path II is triggered on a larger scale, it manifests as a magnetar. If further accretion increases the mass, the star can return to the black hole state.

Small-probability Branch—Destabilization Explosion (Core-collapse Supernova): During the decoherence process, a local fluctuation in the vortex network triggers a chain Path II, and the total energy released exceeds the residual gravitational binding energy. This is the product of three independent probabilities—local fluctuation probability × chain propagation probability × binding energy overcoming probability—which is far less than 1. When the conditions are met, the entire vortex core disintegrates, and the frozen vortex twist energy is released in a concentrated manner in an extremely short time. Observational correspondence: Type II, Type Ib/c core-collapse supernovae. The ejected material returns to the nebula, closing the cycle. The vast majority of black holes smoothly transition to the woven solidified state through silent decoherence; supernovae are a small-probability complication during decoherence.

Final Destination—Woven Solidified Remnant:

The woven solidified state continues to cool. Path II is permanently stopped, and only the unobservable Path I operates at an extremely low rate. The star emits no light at all and becomes a cold solid core.

Complete Cycle Closure:

At any stage of the evolutionary tree, changes in external matter supply can lead to path migration. If a woven solidified state body is in a binary system, accreting material from the companion star can increase its mass and allow it to re-enter the active stage. Supernova ejecta return to the nebula, starting a new round of gravitational condensation. There is no true "finale," only the dynamical switching between Path I and Path II at different stages and different scales. A star is a transient state in the accretion-dissipation cycle.

7.3 Compact Topological Stars (Pulsars): Path I Accumulation → Periodic Triggering of Path II
When the energy released by Path II is insufficient to completely blow the star apart, the remnant is compressed to the extreme under gravity. In this extreme compression state, the sphere of topological knots is pressed to the limit, and the surface structure collapses—the defect area ratio approaches 100%, and the entire star is in a state where "the entire surface can undergo reconnection."

Definition of compact topological star: a compact aggregate of topological knots compressed to a defect ratio near 100%, where the distinction of "stable nuclides" no longer exists. In the compression limit state, the surface weaving of the topological knots has been completely destroyed, and all nodes are in a connection-saturated state—every edge in the network has reached its maximum coordination number and cannot accept new connections. This is connection-saturation rigidity—the topological counterpart of "degeneracy pressure" in the phenomenal layer.

Pulsar Emission Mechanism—Stress Accumulation-Trigger-Release Cycle:

Phase I: Path I continuously operates in the solid network. A very tiny fraction of nuclides independently reach reaction conditions locally, releasing minute amounts of heat and stress. The total number of particles is enormous, and the overall effect of these events makes the stress in the central local region of the star continuously approach the E_comp threshold.

Phase II: When the stress accumulation in the core region reaches E_comp, Path II is triggered—connecting edges are batch-cut, and chain reconnection initiates. Only a small fraction of nuclides in the high-energy core region need reach the critical point. Path II releases a shock wave in a millisecond timescale. The propagation speed of the shock wave in the solid network is determined by the medium's sound speed, estimated to be on the order of 0.3c to c. The radius of a neutron star is about 10 km, and the shock wave traverses the entire star in about 0.03–0.1 ms.

Phase III: The shock wave propagates across the entire sphere in the solid network. The wavefront is extremely narrow and the duration passing through a given location is very short. When the shock wave reaches the surface layer, the wavefront compresses the surface nuclides, and the energy exceeds the instantaneous bearing limit of the surface layer, producing a "spalling" effect—charged fragments are ejected. The observer is extremely far from the star and can only receive signals from a very narrow line-of-sight direction.

Phase IV: After the pulse release, the star returns to Path I, re-accumulating stress, and a cycle is formed.

Proof of Period Stability by the Law of Large Numbers: Millisecond pulsars (P ~1 ms) require timing precision with relative period fluctuations not exceeding 10⁻¹⁵. The total number of particles in the entire star is about 10⁵⁷, and the number of Path I events in a single period far exceeds 10³⁰. The Poisson statistical relative fluctuation is far below the femtosecond level. The femtosecond-level period stability is an inevitable result of the Law of Large Numbers.

Glitches (sudden period changes) correspond to an abnormal stress concentration in a certain region of the core, triggering a larger-scale Path II, with local structural reorganization and a sudden change in the stress accumulation rate or trigger threshold. Magnetars correspond to an even larger scale of Path II triggering, with higher shock wave energy radiating into the X-ray/gamma-ray bands. The death line has no abrupt boundary—continuous mass loss gradually lengthens the accumulation time, the shock wave intensity weakens, intermittency increases, and eventually Path II is no longer triggered at all, and the pulses stop. After stopping, Path I still operates at an extremely low rate, producing weak thermal radiation.

7.4 Topological Stars (Phenomenal "Black Holes")
A aggregate of topological knots whose weaving activity is nearly frozen. Path II is completely silent—the internal stress is continuously below the E_comp threshold of all nuclides, and forced chain reconnection is no longer triggered. Path I is mathematically never zero, but its event rate can be suppressed far below the observational window of the current age of the universe, appearing macroscopically as "silent."

The core of a topological star is a globally phase-locked superfluid vortex condensate (D_ijΘ = 0). The energy released by Path I and Path II is locked into vortex twist energy through a three-step mechanism: Step 1, reconnection events produce local stress shock waves (thermal phonon intermediate state); Step 2, outward escape is captured by the radial shared-link gradient potential well, and inward dissipation is blocked by the lack of dangling ports due to connection saturation; Step 3, phonons accumulate to a nonlinear threshold and are converted into quantized topological vortices through collective reconnection of the shared link network. All released energy is frozen in the form of vortex twist energy, producing no normal optical radiation.

Topological stars do not rely on singularities or event horizons. They are the exposed inner core after the envelope of a camouflage-state star dissipates, a stage in the accretion-dissipation cycle, not an endpoint of evolution.

7.5 Quasars: Imprisoned Internal Circulation Reconnection Storms
Quasars are the brightest objects in the universe, with typical luminosities reaching 10⁴⁰–10⁴¹ W and light variation timescales as short as a few days. The chain reconnection waves of Path II in the core of early-universe primordial topological knot clumps are blocked by the convergence effect of the peripheral massive shared links, unable to penetrate the clump boundary, forming persistent internal circulation reconnection storms.

Quasars do not go through a phenomenal main-sequence stellar progenitor stage. In the complete life cycle picture of TDMU, quasars correspond to the "full-load active" stage—their core is an already-formed compact topological star, and the envelope is a thick accretion layer supplied by a high accretion rate.

7.6 The Shared-Link Origin of Planetary Magnetic Fields
The generation of a planetary magnetic field does not require specific material composition; it is the geometric result of the shared link network achieving directional alignment on a planetary scale. Path I continuously releases heat in the planetary core, driving convective motion in the liquid region. Planetary rotation, acting as a global inertial mold, forcibly constrains random thermal convection into large-scale annular vortices symmetric about the planetary axis—this macroscopically ordered fluid is equivalent to the large-scale directional alignment of shared links. According to Definition 9.5, this directional alignment closes end-to-end on the scale of the planetary core and emerges as a stable dipole magnetic field.

The polarity of the magnetic field corresponds to the chirality (left-handed or right-handed helicity) of the shared link network. Geomagnetic reversal is a global chiral phase transition caused by the outer core circulation crossing a topological bifurcation point—when the heat output of Path I in the northern and southern hemispheres accumulates asymmetrically, the asymmetric perturbation is amplified under rotation, forcing the entire shared link network to flip its chirality. The irregularity of reversal intervals stems from local Path II events randomly injecting reverse seed fiber bundles into the circulation. The slow drift of the magnetic poles is the longitudinal precession of the fluid symmetry axis.

A unified test of this mechanism: Venus has a similar mass to Earth but rotates extremely slowly (243 days). The lack of rotational constraints causes fluid motion to be only local random turbulence; shared links cannot close on a large scale, hence there is no intrinsic dipole magnetic field. Jupiter's metallic hydrogen generating a magnetic field proves that magnetism does not depend on iron-nickel composition—the essence of the magnetic field is the directional alignment of shared links, not specific material.

Part VIII: Geometric Explanations of Cosmological Observations
8.1 Cosmic Microwave Background Radiation
The cosmic microwave background radiation is a topological snapshot taken at the critical transition moment when the membrane interface went from violent change to an equilibrium state—the instant when the woven structure was frozen.

Homogeneous Background: The geometric average state of the initial conditions at the moment of membrane emergence. No inflation is needed to explain homogeneity; homogeneity is the starting point of membrane emergence.

Temperature Fluctuations (Anisotropies): Statistical fluctuations of the local shared link density on the interface, locked in at the moment of freezing. Hot spots correspond to slightly higher density, cold spots to slightly lower density.

Multipole Structure of the Angular Power Spectrum: The spatial projection statistics of topological structures of different scales on the interface at the moment of freezing.

8.2 Large-Scale Structure
The initial homogeneity inside the membrane is broken by statistical fluctuations of local reconnection. The fluctuations are amplified by the positive feedback of Path I. The checker-jump path allows cross-order polymerization, accelerating structure formation.

No dark matter halos are needed as gravitational seeds.

8.3 Galactic Rotation Curves
The gravitational effect of galactic dark matter halos originates from the shared-link convergence effect of supermassive topological stars (black hole class, mass >10⁵ M_⊙). Such objects are extremely few in number and enormously massive; their microlensing timescales are extremely long (on the order of years), and current survey data cannot exclude them as the main component of dark matter halos.

Key Distinction:

Low-mass compact objects (<30 M_⊙) correspond to the solid woven solidified state and do not constitute the bulk of dark matter halos.

Supermassive compact objects (>10⁵ M_⊙) correspond to the fluid vortex state and constitute the bulk of dark matter halos.

There is a natural mass gap between the two categories, determined by topological phase transition conditions.

Response regarding dynamical friction: Long-range topological stress fields are generated between supermassive topological stars due to the shared-link convergence effect. When two topological stars get too close, the shared links reach connection saturation, producing repulsive bow stress that prevents them from sinking into the galactic center. This freezes the supermassive topological stars at various positions in the halo during the early stages of galactic evolution, forming an extended mass distribution.

8.4 Accelerated Expansion of the Universe
The topological tension coupling between the membrane and the external substrate continuously injects energy as the membrane expands, driving the accelerated expansion.

In the phenomenal layer, this continuous energy injection manifests as the accelerated expansion of the universe—the observational effect attributed to dark energy in the standard model. No new field or new entity is needed.

8.5 Elemental Abundance Distribution
The cyclic dynamics (polymerization-fission cycle) of the checker-jump path, integrated over time, produces the observed elemental abundance distribution. Each particle is a transient in time and probability.

8.6 High-Redshift Massive Structures
The checker-jump path allows the direct polymerization of massive topological stars in the early membrane. No seed black holes plus long-timescale accretion are needed. This provides a natural explanation for the high-redshift (z>10) massive galaxies observed by JWST.

8.7 Topological Coupling Mechanisms of a Multi-Membrane Universe
The ontological status of the substrate (Postulate 1) dictates that the membrane is not the only possible structure. If the loop density exceeds the critical value in another region of the substrate, a membrane can spontaneously emerge there as well. This implies that multiple membranes—multiple universes—can exist in the substrate, each emerging independently and each possessing an internal woven state and a shared link network.

8.7.1 Basic Constraints on Inter-Membrane Interactions

The region of substrate between two membranes is in an unwoven state. In the unwoven substrate, there is no shared link network, so light (δ𝒜) cannot propagate between two membranes. There is no usual "communication by light" between membranes.

But this does not mean membranes are completely isolated from each other. The unwoven substrate still consists of nodes and directed edges and still obeys local reconnection dynamics. Any violent dynamical process in one membrane will generate local reconnection perturbations in the substrate. These perturbations propagate in the substrate as reconnection event waves and may reach another membrane.

8.7.2 Three Channels of Inter-Membrane Interaction

Channel I: Direct Impact of Substrate Reconnection Shock Waves

Any violent dynamical process in another external membrane—Path II events inside it (supernova explosions or core collapse), or global oscillations of the membrane itself—will generate local reconnection perturbations in the substrate. These perturbations are not light but reconnection event waves of the substrate.

When a reconnection event wave reaches the surface of our universe's membrane, it will locally interact with the topological defects (Definition 7.2) on the membrane surface. Because the membrane surface is the interface between the woven and unwoven states, the surface topological defects are the only sites capable of interacting with external perturbations. Observable manifestations of this interaction include:

Collective excitation of membrane surface defects, manifesting as coherence anomalies in the cosmic microwave background that cannot be explained by internal fluctuations.

Energy injection in specific directions, possibly triggering local weaving restructuring on the membrane surface, manifesting in the phenomenal layer as diffuse gamma-ray bursts or X-ray flashes unattributable to known sources.

It must be noted that reconnection shock waves of the substrate, when propagating in the unwoven state, rapidly disperse due to the lack of a coherent medium, and their intensity decays exponentially with distance. This means that only an external membrane sufficiently close to our membrane can produce an observable impact event.

Channel II: Indirect Influence of Topological Tension Coupling

Topological tension coupling exists between the membrane and the external substrate (an extension of Postulate 5). If another massively large membrane exists externally, its own topological tension will alter the substrate stress field in the region where our membrane resides.

Anisotropy in the substrate stress field will cause anisotropy in the accelerated expansion rate of our universe—in different directions, there exist tiny systematic differences in the Hubble constant. Such differences are not random but should exhibit a large-scale dipole or quadrupole pattern related to the direction of the external membrane.

Current cosmological observations have already discovered the so-called "Hubble tension"—significant differences in the Hubble constant measured by different methods. TDMU attributes this to the anisotropic stress from external topological structures, rather than measurement systematic errors or new physics. This interpretation yields a clear observational prediction: the directional dependence of the Hubble constant should exhibit a systematic pattern rather than a random distribution.

Channel III: Topological Defect Handshake between Membranes—An Extremely Rare Large-Scale Coupling

If two membranes approach close enough that their surface topological defects can come into direct contact, defect-complementary weaving between the two membrane surfaces can occur.

Defect-complementary weaving is the basic mechanism for interactions between topological knots in TDMU (Path I). The same mechanism can operate between the surface defects of two membranes. When the defects on the surfaces of two membranes happen to be geometrically complementary, they mesh to form a shared link "bridge" connecting the two membranes.

The establishment of this bridge is an instantaneous phase transition event. Afterward, a shared link passage will exist between the two membranes for the first time. Light (δ𝒜) can propagate along this bridge from one membrane to another. Observable manifestations of such an event in the phenomenal layer may include:

In an extremely short time, a certain region of the universe suddenly experiences an ultra-high-energy radiation burst unexplainable by any known astrophysical process.

Accompanying the burst, the fine-structure constant or the speed of light in that region exhibits an instantaneous, measurable deviation—because the shared link networks of the two membranes briefly overlapped, altering the local shared link network properties.

If the bridge persists stably, it will become a permanent channel connecting the two membranes. In the phenomenal layer, this corresponds to the concept of a wormhole in standard cosmology, but its ontological foundation is entirely different: a wormhole is not a connection in spacetime topology, but a shared link bridge between two woven membranes.

8.7.3 The Multi-Membrane Universe and the Ultimate Constraint on Civilizational Persistence

The multi-membrane coupling mechanism of TDMU provides a physical formulation for an ancient philosophical question: can a civilization persist after the dissolution of its parent membrane?

From Postulate 12, we know that any membrane—whether naturally emerged or artificially constructed—will eventually dissolve. The dissolution of a membrane means the collapse of all woven structures within it, including all topological knots, all shared link networks, and all matter and life based on these structures. There are no exceptions.

Therefore, for a civilization to exceed the lifespan limit of a single membrane, it must master the technology of inter-membrane migration. This includes two possible paths:

Path I: Construct an artificial membrane (Ark). Before the dissolution of the parent membrane, a civilization can use the matter and energy within the parent membrane to build a child membrane that independently maintains weaving stability. The child membrane survives the shock wave of the parent membrane's dissolution, then navigates through the substrate, searching for another natural membrane and establishing a shared link bridge with it. The technical challenges of this path have been discussed in Part VII.

Path II: Find an existing external membrane and establish a connection in advance. If a civilization can detect a neighboring external membrane and establish a shared link bridge with it before the parent membrane dissolves, it can transfer its population and information to the external membrane when the parent membrane dissolves. This path avoids the engineering challenges of building a child membrane but requires extremely stringent conditions: the external membrane must be at a sufficiently close distance, its weaving pattern must be compatible with the parent membrane, and its surface defects must be capable of defect-complementary weaving with those on the parent membrane surface.

Whichever path is chosen, the civilization faces a fundamental limitation: there is no shared link network in the substrate, so light cannot travel between membranes. During inter-membrane migration, the civilization will be in a state of complete information blindness—they cannot use light to perceive the external environment, cannot know in advance the internal state of the destination membrane, and cannot communicate with any external entities during the voyage. They can only perceive the world by touching the tremors of the substrate itself.

This is the deepest constraint that TDMU gives to civilization: the membrane is an oasis of existence, and the substrate is a boundless desert. The journey across the desert is blind, lonely, and irreversible. The ultimate fate of a civilization depends not on its degree of development within a single membrane, but on whether it can learn to continuously migrate across the succession of membrane generations—before each membrane dissolves, building the next ark, finding the next home.

There is no endpoint. There is only the cycle. Meaning is not conserved; only reconnection is ceaseless.

Part IX: Testable Predictions
Below are listed testable predictions of the TDMU framework. Each is phrased in an "If...then..." format to ensure falsifiability.

Prediction 9.1: Compact Object Mass Gap
Prediction: There should be a lack of stable compact objects in the phenomenal range of approximately 2.5–5 M_⊙. Any objects found in this range should exhibit transitional behavior. If a large number of stable compact objects are found in this range, the framework needs revision.

Prediction 9.2: Solid Planet Inertia Upper Limit
Prediction: Purely solid planets exceeding a certain critical inertia cannot exist stably. If numerous long-term stable purely solid planets are found in any inertia range, the framework is falsified.

Prediction 9.3: Asteroseismological Test for Extreme Stars
Prediction: The seismological mass M_seismo of extreme stars should be significantly larger than their photometric mass M_lum, with the ratio M_seismo/M_lum > 1.5, and should monotonically increase with radius. If the ratio is approximately equal to 1, the hidden core hypothesis does not hold.

Prediction 9.4: Flux Quantization Structure
Prediction: Magnetic flux must be an integer multiple of some irreducible elementary unit. If any superconducting system observes magnetic flux that is not an integer multiple of an irreducible elementary unit, the framework is falsified.

Prediction 9.5: Impact Imprints of External Membranes
Prediction: If there exist large-scale coherent structures in the cosmic microwave background that cannot be explained by internal fluctuations, and these structures are concentrated in specific sky regions, they may be topological defect excitation events from an external membrane. If such structures are absent in higher-precision CMB surveys, this channel is inactive in the current observational window.

Prediction 9.6: Anisotropic Expansion
Prediction: If the Hubble constant exhibits systematic differences exceeding statistical errors across different sky regions, and the distribution of differences shows a large-scale dipole or quadrupole pattern, there may exist topological tension coupling from external cosmic membranes. If the directional dependence of the Hubble constant disappears under higher precision, this prediction is falsified.

Prediction 9.7: Transient Signals of Inter-Membrane Bridging
Prediction: If ultra-high-energy radiation bursts unexplainable by any known astrophysical process are observed, and accompanying these bursts are instantaneous, localized, measurable deviations of the fine-structure constant or the speed of light, they may correspond to a topological defect handshake event between two membranes. If all such bursts do not accompany instantaneous deviations of these physical constants, this channel is not activated in the observational window.

Part X: Retrospect
The entire content of physics—space, time, matter, gravity, magnetism, electricity, nuclear force, weak force, electric current, superconductivity, nuclear transformations, elemental abundance, astrophysical evolution, the topological phase transition origin and finale of the membrane, the geometric enforcement of quantum statistics, the geometric decoupling of isotopic fission, the topological origin of superconducting gauge fields—all reduce to three things:

Nodes, directed edges, shared links.

Plus two emergent principles:

Recursive weaving and defect complementarity.

And two truncation rules:

The Hierarchical Truncation Criterion (the geometric window of effective interaction is determined by the recursive growth radius) and the Energy-Scale Truncation Rule (recursive growth is truncated at the highest weaving complexity that can stably exist at the current loop density).

Theorem 8.1 re-anchors Pauli exclusion—one of the most central statistical rules in quantum field theory—from the mysterious result of the "spin-statistics theorem" onto the geometric enforcement of braiding exchange. Two identical topological knots cannot occupy exactly the same geometric state, not because they obey Fermi-Dirac statistics, but because exchanging their ports requires an odd number of reconnection steps, while the identity operation requires zero steps (even)—the contradiction that odd equals even forbids the complete coincidence of states at the purely combinatorial level.

There are no absolute "elementary particles," only eternal recursion and emergence—where recursion is truncated by the energy scale, there "elementary particles" manifest. There is no true "finale," only the dynamical switching of Path I and Path II in the accretion-dissipation cycle. Stars are not unidirectional arrows pointing to an endpoint, but transient states in a cyclic network—brown dwarfs, main-sequence stars, extreme stars, white dwarfs, black holes, pulsars, supernovae—all are different projections of the same set of geometric mechanisms onto the three coordinates of total accreted mass, medium state, and evolutionary stage.

In Part VIII, we went even further: our membrane—our universe—may not be the only self-organized vortex in the substrate. There may be other membranes, each with its own internal spacetime and physics, each undergoing emergence and dissolution. Between membranes there is no communication by light—light is only ripples on the shared link network inside a membrane, unable to traverse the unwoven substrate. But they are not completely isolated from one another. The tremors of the substrate itself convey each other's existence. If a membrane forms or dies sufficiently close, our universe will feel a silent ripple across the topological sea—not seen in light, but heard in the rhythm of expansion.

At the bottom of a summer night pond, a toad rests quietly in the water, blowing bubbles from its mouth. The bubbles rise, expand, deform, oscillate, and finally burst, returning to the water.

In this image:

The water is the substrate—that invisible underlying layer that carries everything. There may be other bubbles in the water, each independently rising, expanding, and bursting. There is no direct sound transmission between bubbles, but they are all in the same water, and their tremors are transmitted through the water.

The bubble wall is the cosmic membrane—ordered structure emerging from the substrate.

The rising of the bubble is time—irreversible unfolding.

The expansion of the bubble is the cooling and evolution of the universe.

The texture of the bubble is matter—woven structures on the membrane, their complexity truncated by the current energy scale.

The bursting of the bubble is the finale—structures disintegrate, returning to the substrate.

The toad still rests quietly in the unreachable depths of the substrate. We are born on the ripples of the membrane and can never turn back to gaze upon it—but that does not prevent us from reconstructing its form step by step through the topological imprints on the membrane.

Why do I keep this image? The toad blowing bubbles was the starting point of my model. Through many rounds of iterative deepening, I cannot abandon it. Without its imagery, none of this would exist. Therefore I still keep the name "Toad Universe Model." Including my topological stars, I still prefer to call them "Toad Stars."

Meaning is not conserved; only reconnection is ceaseless.

